Notebook[{Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["Young Tableaux and Tensor Symmetries", "Title"], Cell[TextData[{"The symmetric group ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[SubscriptBox["S", "n"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " acts on a rank-", Cell[BoxData[FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " tensor by permuting its slots, and the resulting representation splits into a direct sum of irreducibles labelled by Young tableaux. The ", Cell[BoxData[ButtonBox["Wolfram`TensorNetworks`Symmetry`", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/guide/TensorNetworks"]], "InlineFormula"], " subpackage exposes the combinatorial layer (tableaux, hook lengths, dimensions) together with the projectors that decompose a tensor into its irreducible pieces. This tutorial walks through the data structure, the hook-length formula, and the Young projector on small explicit examples."}], "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"PacletDirectoryLoad", "[", "\"/Users/mohammadb/Documents/GitHub/TensorNetworks\"", "]"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Needs", "[", "\"Wolfram`TensorNetworks`\"", "]"}]], "Input"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"Needs", "[", "\"Wolfram`TensorNetworks`Symmetry`\"", "]"}]], "Input"], Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["1. Young Tableaux and Symmetry Classes", "Section"], Cell[TextData[{"A Young diagram of size ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " is a partition of ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " into rows of non-increasing length, drawn as a left-justified array of boxes. Filling those boxes with the slot indices ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"1", ",", " ", "\[Ellipsis]", ",", " ", "n"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " gives a standard Young tableau, which the paclet stores as ", Cell[BoxData[ButtonBox["YoungTableau", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/YoungTableau"]], "InlineFormula"], ". Every standard tableau labels an irreducible representation of ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[SubscriptBox["S", "n"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " acting on rank-", Cell[BoxData[FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " tensors."}], "Text"], Cell[TextData[{"Two extremes are familiar. The single-row shape ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"{", "n", "}"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " corresponds to the fully symmetric tensors, the single-column shape ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"1", ",", "1", ",", "\[Ellipsis]", ",", "1"}], "}"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " to the fully antisymmetric ones, and the mixed shapes label all the intermediate irreps. The constructor accepts either an explicit filling or just a partition (auto-filled left-to-right, top-to-bottom)."}], "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"yt", "=", RowBox[{"YoungTableau", "[", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"3", ",", "2"}], "}"}], "]"}]}]], "Input"], Cell[TextData[{"The shape ", Cell[BoxData[ButtonBox["TableauShape", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/TableauShape"]], "InlineFormula"], ", total number of boxes ", Cell[BoxData[ButtonBox["TableauSize", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/TableauSize"]], "InlineFormula"], ", and validity predicate ", Cell[BoxData[ButtonBox["YoungTableauQ", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/YoungTableauQ"]], "InlineFormula"], " read off directly from the data structure."}], "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"TableauShape", "[", "yt", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"TableauSize", "[", "yt", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"YoungTableauQ", "[", "yt", "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Input"]}, Open]], Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["2. The Hook-Length Formula", "Section"], Cell[TextData[{"The hook of a cell ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"i", ",", "j"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " is the set of cells lying to its right in row ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox["i", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " together with the cells below it in column ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox["j", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], ", plus the cell itself. Frame and James' hook-length formula expresses the dimension of the corresponding ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[SubscriptBox["S", "n"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " irrep as"}], "Text"], Cell[TextData[Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{SubscriptBox["d", "\[Lambda]"], "=", FractionBox[RowBox[{"n", "!"}], RowBox[{UnderscriptBox["\[Product]", RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"i", ",", "j"}], ")"}]], " ", RowBox[{"h", "(", RowBox[{"i", ",", "j"}], ")"}]}]]}], TraditionalForm]], FormatType -> TraditionalForm]], "Text"], Cell[TextData[{"where ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"h", "(", RowBox[{"i", ",", "j"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " is the hook length at cell ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"i", ",", "j"}], ")"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], ". ", Cell[BoxData[ButtonBox["HookLengths", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/HookLengths"]], "InlineFormula"], " returns all hook lengths arranged in the tableau shape, and ", Cell[BoxData[ButtonBox["HookLength", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/HookLength"]], "InlineFormula"], " picks out a single cell."}], "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"HookLengths", "[", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"3", ",", "2"}], "}"}], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"HookLength", "[", RowBox[{"yt", ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"1", ",", "1"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Input"], Cell[TextData[{"Multiplying the hook lengths and dividing into ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"n", "!"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " gives ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{FractionBox[RowBox[{"5", "!"}], RowBox[{"4", "\[CenterDot]", "3", "\[CenterDot]", "1", "\[CenterDot]", "2", "\[CenterDot]", "1"}]], "=", "5"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], ", which is what ", Cell[BoxData[ButtonBox["TableauDimension", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/TableauDimension"]], "InlineFormula"], " reports. Internally the paclet does not loop over the ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " cells; it uses the Frobenius determinant form, which is ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"O", "(", SuperscriptBox["r", "3"], ")"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " in the number of rows ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox["r", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " rather than ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"O", "(", SuperscriptBox["n", "2"], ")"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " in the box count. The reciprocal of the hook product is exposed directly as ", Cell[BoxData[ButtonBox["HookFactor", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/HookFactor"]], "InlineFormula"], ", with the identity ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{RowBox[{"TableauDimension", "[", "\[Lambda]", "]"}], "=", RowBox[{RowBox[{"n", "!"}], "\[CenterDot]", RowBox[{"HookFactor", "[", "\[Lambda]", "]"}]}]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], "."}], "Text"], Cell[TextData[{"Two partition-level utilities round out the combinatorics. ", Cell[BoxData[ButtonBox["PartitionQ", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/PartitionQ"]], "InlineFormula"], " tests whether a list of integers is a partition (positive and non-increasing), and ", Cell[BoxData[ButtonBox["TransposePartition", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/TransposePartition"]], "InlineFormula"], " conjugates a Young diagram by swapping its rows and columns. The dimensions of conjugate partitions agree, since transposing a diagram turns the symmetrizer into the antisymmetrizer and vice versa."}], "Text"], Cell[BoxData[RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"TableauDimension", "[", "yt", "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"HookFactor", "[", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"3", ",", "2"}], "}"}], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"PartitionQ", "[", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"3", ",", "2"}], "}"}], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"PartitionQ", "[", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"2", ",", "3"}], "}"}], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"TransposePartition", "[", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"4", ",", "2", ",", "1"}], "}"}], "]"}]}], "}"}]], "Input"]}, Open]], Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["3. Projecting Tensors", "Section"], Cell[TextData[{"The Young symmetrizer associated with a tableau ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox["T", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " is the algebra element ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{SubscriptBox["c", "T"], "=", RowBox[{SubscriptBox["a", "T"], "\[CenterDot]", SubscriptBox["b", "T"]}]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], ", where ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[SubscriptBox["b", "T"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " symmetrizes the slots in each row of ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox["T", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " and ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[SubscriptBox["a", "T"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " antisymmetrizes the slots in each column. The paclet applies them in exactly that order \[Dash] rows first, then columns \[Dash] which is the order that leaves the final column antisymmetry intact. ", Cell[BoxData[ButtonBox["YoungSymmetrize", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/YoungSymmetrize"]], "InlineFormula"], " evaluates ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{SubscriptBox["c", "T"], "[", "T", "]"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " on a tensor whose rank equals the tableau size."}], "Text"], Cell[TextData[{"The unnormalized symmetrizer satisfies ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{SuperscriptBox[SubscriptBox["c", "T"], "2"], "=", RowBox[{FractionBox[RowBox[{"n", "!"}], SubscriptBox["d", "\[Lambda]"]], SubscriptBox["c", "T"]}]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], ", so the genuine projector onto the irrep is ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{SubscriptBox["P", "T"], "=", RowBox[{FractionBox[SubscriptBox["d", "\[Lambda]"], RowBox[{"n", "!"}]], SubscriptBox["c", "T"]}]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], ". This is what ", Cell[BoxData[ButtonBox["YoungProject", BaseStyle -> "Link", ButtonData -> "paclet:Wolfram/TensorNetworks/ref/YoungProject"]], "InlineFormula"], " applies, with the normalization chosen so that ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["P", "2"], "=", "P"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], "."}], "Text"], Cell[TextData[{"The rank-2 case is the most familiar sanity check: shape ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"{", "2", "}"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " projects onto the symmetric part ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{FractionBox["1", "2"], RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"T", "+", SuperscriptBox["T", "\[Transpose]"]}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], ", and shape ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"1", ",", "1"}], "}"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " onto the antisymmetric part ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{FractionBox["1", "2"], RowBox[{"(", RowBox[{"T", "-", SuperscriptBox["T", "\[Transpose]"]}], ")"}]}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], ". A symbolic ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"2", "\[Times]", "2"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " tensor confirms both."}], "Text"], Cell[BoxData[{RowBox[{RowBox[{"T", "=", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"a", ",", "b"}], "}"}], ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"c", ",", "d"}], "}"}]}], "}"}]}], ";"}], "\n", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{RowBox[{"YoungProject", "[", RowBox[{"T", ",", RowBox[{"YoungTableau", "[", RowBox[{"{", "2", "}"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], ",", RowBox[{"YoungProject", "[", RowBox[{"T", ",", RowBox[{"YoungTableau", "[", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"1", ",", "1"}], "}"}], "]"}]}], "]"}]}], "}"}]}], "Input"], Cell[TextData[{"At rank three the new feature is the mixed-symmetry irrep ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"2", ",", "1"}], "}"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], ", whose representation is two-dimensional. A rank-3 tensor decomposes into the symmetric piece (shape ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"{", "3", "}"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], ", dimension 1), the antisymmetric piece (shape ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"1", ",", "1", ",", "1"}], "}"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], ", dimension 1), and two copies of the mixed irrep (one for each standard filling of ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"2", ",", "1"}], "}"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], ", giving total dimension 2). Projecting twice with ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{"YoungProject", "[", RowBox[{"\[CenterEllipsis]", ",", RowBox[{"YoungTableau", "[", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"2", ",", "1"}], "}"}], "]"}]}], "]"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " should be the same as projecting once."}], "Text"], Cell[BoxData[{RowBox[{RowBox[{"SeedRandom", "[", "42", "]"}], ";"}], "\n", RowBox[{RowBox[{"R", "=", RowBox[{"Round", "[", RowBox[{RowBox[{"10", " ", RowBox[{"RandomReal", "[", RowBox[{"1", ",", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"2", ",", "2", ",", "2"}], "}"}]}], "]"}]}], ",", "0.01"}], "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", RowBox[{RowBox[{"yt21", "=", RowBox[{"YoungTableau", "[", RowBox[{"{", RowBox[{"2", ",", "1"}], "}"}], "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", RowBox[{RowBox[{"P1", "=", RowBox[{"YoungProject", "[", RowBox[{"R", ",", "yt21"}], "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", RowBox[{RowBox[{"P2", "=", RowBox[{"YoungProject", "[", RowBox[{"P1", ",", "yt21"}], "]"}]}], ";"}], "\n", RowBox[{"Max", "[", RowBox[{"Abs", "[", RowBox[{"Flatten", "[", RowBox[{"P1", "-", "P2"}], "]"}], "]"}], "]"}]}], "Input"], Cell[TextData[{"The residual is numerically zero, confirming ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[RowBox[{SuperscriptBox["P", "2"], "=", "P"}], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], ". Combining the projectors for all shapes of a given size recovers the original tensor, which is the statement that ", Cell[BoxData[FormBox[SubscriptBox["S", "n"], TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " acts completely reducibly on rank-", Cell[BoxData[FormBox["n", TraditionalForm]], "InlineFormula"], " tensors. The combinatorial bookkeeping that controls when each shape contributes \[Dash] tableau dimensions and hook lengths \[Dash] is exactly what the earlier sections compute."}], "Text"]}, Open]], Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["Related Guides", "TutorialMoreAboutSection"], Cell["TensorNetworks", "TutorialMoreAbout"]}, Open]], Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["Related Tech Notes", "RelatedTutorialsSection"], Cell["Tensor Networks Overview", "RelatedTutorials"], Cell["Named Indices and Metrics", "RelatedTutorials"]}, Open]]}, Open]], Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["Metadata", "MetadataSection"], Cell[TextData[{"New in: ", Cell["1.0", "HistoryData", CellTags -> "New"], " | Modified in: ", Cell[" ", "HistoryData", CellTags -> "Modified"], " | Obsolete in: ", Cell[" ", "HistoryData", CellTags -> "Obsolete"]}], "History"], Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["Categorization", "CategorizationSection"], Cell["Tech Note", "Categorization", CellLabel -> "Entity Type"], Cell["Wolfram/TensorNetworks", "Categorization", CellLabel -> "Paclet Name"], Cell["Wolfram`TensorNetworks`Symmetry`", "Categorization", CellLabel -> "Context"], Cell["Wolfram/TensorNetworks/tutorial/YoungSymmetries", "Categorization", CellLabel -> "URI"]}, Closed]], Cell[CellGroupData[{Cell["Keywords", "KeywordsSection"], Cell["Young tableau, symmetric group, hook length formula, irreducible representation, symmetrization, antisymmetrization", "Keywords"]}, Closed]]}, Open]]}, WindowSize -> {840, 900}, WindowMargins -> {{Automatic, 200}, {36, Automatic}}, TaggingRules -> <|"Paclet" -> "Wolfram/TensorNetworks"|>, FrontEndVersion -> "15.0 for Mac OS X ARM (64-bit) (November 18, 2025)", StyleDefinitions -> FrontEnd`FileName[{"Wolfram"}, "TechNotePageStylesExt.nb", CharacterEncoding -> "UTF-8"]]
